gm-method (landing a 'deferred-with-rationale, optimize X' item the user later asks to land): the deferral row's stated optimization is a HYPOTHESIS, not a spec -- read the REAL hot fn first, because the imagined win is often phantom. Concrete pattern: a row claimed a server priority-selection did an O(R log R) sort that a top-K heap would replace; the real fn had NO sort (already O(R) accumulate + bucket-select), so the heap was phantom churn. But MEASURING it (a bounded real-module load harness: drive the REAL exported fn over a REAL cache at N=50..500 x hundreds of iterations, record p50/p95/p99 via process.hrtime) exposed the ACTUAL removable cost: per-call throwaway array allocation (`const buckets=[[],[],[],[]]`) driving a GC p99 tail (2558us@N500). Fix = hoist to module-scope arrays cleared per call (valid because single-threaded + non-re-entrant); re-measure witnessed 6.8x p99 drop + scale-ratio 23x->5.4x. LESSON: resolve the optimization row by the harness's numbers -- land the real measured win, reject the phantom one WITH the p99 numbers as witness, never churn working code on the row's assumption. SECOND lesson (test surface): a load/chaos/p99 witness is admissible as a SECOND root-level real-module file (e.g. test-load.js sibling to test.js) -- mock-free, single file, NOT a unit-test tree -- when it asserts a distinct invariant class (Crucible max-load / profiling) that would blow the correctness witness's line cap; guard it against .gitignore's `test-*.js` scratch-glob with a `!test-load.js` negation or it silently never commits. THIRD: when adding public accessors to replace cross-module private-field reaches, the setters must write the SAME backing fields the existing tests assert, AND the test MOCKS of that class must mirror the new accessor methods (else production setters throw 'X is not a function' in the test) -- the mock is part of the contract surface.